The Commission accepted Respondent's offer of settlement this morning and e=
ntered the settled order. Copy attached.<br />
<br />
Marchwood is censured, ordered to cease and desist, and ordered to pay disg=
orgement of $1,412,000, prejudgment interest of $137,000 and a civil penalt=
y of $601,000, for a total of $2,150,000, together with undertakings that t=
his time are recited in a Commission order rather than in a letter. The fee=
 application is withdrawn and there is no award.<br />
<br />
Three things for the record while they are fresh.<br />
<br />
The case was won by a letter the respondent wrote us in 2011 and by a lette=
r the respondent wrote us in 2013, neither of which was in either party's d=
ocument system, and both of which were in the email journal archive the who=
le time - through the 2016 migration, through two changes of counsel, throu=
gh the retirement of every person who negotiated them. Han-Wei's team ran t=
he search in a day. We had been looking for nine weeks in the one place the=
 documents were guaranteed not to be.<br />
<br />
We produced a record to Respondent in May that supported Respondent's posit=
ion and did not support ours, and we did that in good faith because it was =
what our case system held. Respondent's counsel was entitled to rely on it =
and did. That is a disclosure problem, not a filing problem, and it will ha=
ppen again on every pre-2014 matter until somebody fixes the underlying rec=
ord.<br />
<br />
And Yolanda's question from September 2023 has an answer now. We did not mi=
ss it in 2011. It came back, after the compliance officer who gave the unde=
rtaking left, on a firm whose risk score said there was nothing here becaus=
e the disposition code said there was nothing here.<br />
<br />
Han-Wei, I would like the three of you to write this up.<br />
<br />
Camille Ostrowski<br />
Assistant Director, Division of Enforcement
